# Binary MCP Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values
# Environment variables override .env file values

# VIRUSTOTAL
# Get your API key from: https://www.virustotal.com/gui/my-apikey
VT_API_KEY=your_virustotal_api_key_here

# Socket timeout for VirusTotal calls in seconds (default: 30, clamped 5-300)
# VT_API_TIMEOUT=30

# ABUSE.CH (MalwareBazaar, ThreatFox, URLhaus, YARAify)
# ONE Auth-Key covers all four services, and it is MANDATORY for every one of
# them -- abuse.ch removed anonymous access on 30 June 2025, so a client
# without a key gets no data at all rather than an obvious error. This is the
# single most common reason an older abuse.ch client suddenly returns nothing.
# Keys are free: https://auth.abuse.ch/
ABUSECH_API_KEY=your_abusech_auth_key_here

# MB_API_KEY is still read as an alias, because it shipped before the
# service-neutral name existed. ABUSECH_API_KEY wins when both are set.
# MB_API_KEY=your_abusech_auth_key_here

# Socket timeouts in seconds (default: 30, clamped 5-300)
# MB_API_TIMEOUT=30
# ABUSECH_API_TIMEOUT=30

# MITRE ATT&CK
# No API key and no rate limit -- ATT&CK is published as static STIX bundles.
# The bundle is fetched once (~51MB for Enterprise), reduced to a ~2.7MB index,
# and served from disk after that, so every lookup past the first is offline.
#
# Matrix: enterprise-attack (default), mobile-attack, ics-attack
# ATTACK_DOMAIN=enterprise-attack
#
# Where the distilled index is cached (default: $BINARY_CACHE_DIR/attack)
# ATTACK_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/binary-mcp/attack
#
# Refuse to download; serve only what is already cached (air-gapped analysis)
# ATTACK_OFFLINE=1
#
# Ceiling on the downloaded bundle in MB (default: 128, clamped 8-1024)
# ATTACK_MAX_BUNDLE_MB=128
#
# Socket timeout for the download in seconds (default: 120, clamped 5-600)
# ATTACK_TIMEOUT=120

# Enable mb_download. This is the ONLY tool in this server that writes malware
# to disk. Archives land in ~/.binary_mcp_output/malwarebazaar/ as abuse.ch's
# password-protected zip ("infected") and are never extracted by the server --
# extract them in an isolated analysis VM, not on this host.
# MB_ALLOW_DOWNLOAD=1

# Ceiling on a downloaded archive, in MB (default: 128, clamped 1-2048)
# MB_MAX_DOWNLOAD_MB=128

# GHIDRA
# Path to Ghidra installation (auto-detected if not set)
# GHIDRA_HOME=/opt/ghidra

# Analysis timeout in seconds (default: 1800)
# GHIDRA_TIMEOUT=1800

# JVM max heap for the Ghidra subprocess, in MB (default: 4096)
# Bump to 6144-8192 for very large binaries -- a full-depth run holds every
# function AND every pseudocode body in memory before serialising, which is
# where a multi-MB DLL with 20K+ functions runs out of heap.
# Applied via _JAVA_OPTIONS, which the JVM parses after the command line, so
# it overrides the -Xmx Ghidra's own launcher passes. The exception: an -Xmx
# ALREADY present in _JAVA_OPTIONS wins and is left alone, so a global
# "-Xmx2g" in your environment silently caps Ghidra regardless of the value
# below. Check `echo $_JAVA_OPTIONS` first if Ghidra OOMs despite raising this.
# GHIDRA_MAX_HEAP_MB=4096

# X64DBG
# HTTP bridge URL (default: http://localhost:27042)
# X64DBG_BRIDGE_URL=http://localhost:27042

# Command timeout in seconds (default: 30)
# X64DBG_TIMEOUT=30

# STORAGE
# Cache directory for analysis results
# BINARY_MCP_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/binary-mcp/cache

# Session storage directory
# BINARY_MCP_SESSION_DIR=/tmp/binary-mcp/sessions

# LOGGING
# Log level: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR
# BINARY_MCP_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
